                <pb facs="0744" n="650" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg650"/>

                <head>No. 402.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Baron Fava to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of Italy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-04-22"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 22, 1887</date>.
                        (Received April 23.)</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary of State</hi>:</p>
                <p>By your note dated to-day you were good enough to transmit me eleven copies of
                    the circular of the honorable the Secretary of the Treasury, dated March 24
                    last, “prohibiting the importation of foreign laborers under contract.”</p>
                <p>I am under great obligations for the transmission of this document, which I have
                    hastened to transmit to my Government, and I have the honor to offer to your
                    excellency, and, through your kind intermediation, to the honorable Mr.
                    Fairchild my thanks.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Fava.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
